barch-rankmirrors
==================

A tool for ranking BlackArch Linux pacman mirrors by actual
reachability and response time, and optionally writing the result
directly to /etc/pacman.d/blackarch-mirrorlist.


WHY
---

BlackArch ships a static mirrorlist with no official ranking tool,
unlike Arch's mirror status page or EndeavourOS's eos-rankmirrors.
Dead or slow mirrors sit at arbitrary positions in the file, which
means `pacman -Syu` can hang or fail on a mirror that has been
unreachable for months.

This tool fetches the current mirrorlist from blackarch.org, tests
every mirror concurrently, ranks the reachable ones by latency, and
writes a curated active block to the top of the mirrorlist while
preserving the full original list below for reference.


USAGE
-----

    barch-rankmirrors [flags]

    Flags:
        --allow-http             also test http:// mirrors, not just https://
        --allow-rsync            also test rsync:// mirrors, via a raw
                                 daemon handshake
        --n <int>                number of top mirrors to output/write (default 5)
        --rank-all               show all reachable mirrors, ignoring --n
                                (display only; --update still uses --n)
        --exclude-country <s>    comma-separated countries to exclude
                                 (e.g. 'China,Russia')
        --country-only <s>       comma-separated countries to include only
                                 (mutually exclusive with --exclude-country)
        --ipv4-only              only test mirrors over IPv4
        --ipv6-only              only test mirrors over IPv6
                                 (mutually exclusive with each other)
        --per-mirror-timeout <d> per-mirror probe timeout (default 5s)
        --output <path>          save whatever is printed to stdout to
                                 this file as well
        --mirrors-only           print only the rendered mirrorlist to
                                 stdout (logs go to stderr); safe to pipe
        --update                 write ranked mirrorlist to
                                 /etc/pacman.d/blackarch-mirrorlist
                                 (requires root)
        --force                  skip the confirmation prompt (only
                                 meaningful with --update)

    Without --update, results print to stdout only. Nothing on disk
    is touched unless you pass --update, and even then you will be
    asked to confirm before anything is written, unless --force is
    given.


EXAMPLES
--------

    barch-rankmirrors
        Test https mirrors, print top 5 to stdout.

    barch-rankmirrors --allow-http --n 10
        Widen to http+https, show top 10.

    barch-rankmirrors --allow-rsync
        Also test rsync:// mirrors alongside https.

    barch-rankmirrors --allow-http --allow-rsync
        Test https, http, and rsync mirrors together.

    barch-rankmirrors --exclude-country China,Russia --n 10
        Exclude specific countries from testing.

    barch-rankmirrors --country-only India,Germany
        Only test mirrors from specific countries.

    barch-rankmirrors --rank-all
        Show every reachable mirror, ranked, ignoring --n.

    barch-rankmirrors --ipv6-only
        Only test mirrors reachable over IPv6.

    barch-rankmirrors --mirrors-only | sudo tee /etc/pacman.d/blackarch-mirrorlist
        Run entirely without root, pipe the ranked mirrorlist
        straight into a privileged write command.

    barch-rankmirrors --output run.log
        Save everything printed to stdout to a file as well.

    sudo barch-rankmirrors --update
        Test, rank, prompt for confirmation, then write.

    sudo barch-rankmirrors --update --force
        Same as above, no prompt.


HOW IT WORKS
------------

    1. fetch   - download the raw mirrorlist from
                 https://www.blackarch.org/blackarch-mirrorlist
    2. parse   - split into per-country groups of Server entries,
                 tracking which lines were already active
    3. filter  - apply --exclude-country / --country-only if set
    4. probe   - concurrently test each candidate mirror, with a
                 20-worker pool and a configurable per-mirror
                 timeout (--per-mirror-timeout). https/http mirrors
                 are HEAD-requested against blackarch.db; rsync
                 mirrors get a raw daemon handshake (greeting,
                 version and digest negotiation, module request) to
                 confirm reachability without a full file sync
    5. refine  - the most promising mirrors from the probe step (up
                 to 3x the requested count) are re-probed 3 times each
                 at higher concurrency, and ranked by the median of
                 those samples rather than a single reading. A mirror
                 that fails every re-probe is dropped as unreachable;
                 one that fails only some attempts is still ranked,
                 but pushed down for the flakiness. --rank-all applies
                 the same refinement to its top 30 mirrors; the rest
                 keep their single-probe latency.
    6. rank    - sort refined mirrors by median response time, clamp
                 to the requested count
    7. render  - write a dated, country-annotated active block,
                 followed by the full original mirrorlist,
                 re-commented, in its original order
    8. write   - (--update only) back up the existing mirrorlist to
                 blackarch-mirrorlist.bak, then write the new file.
                 Backup must succeed before the real file is touched.

No mirror is ever assumed correct or malformed; a bad URL simply
fails the probe step and is excluded from ranking. https and http
are tested via net/http; rsync is tested via a hand-written daemon
handshake (no third-party dependency). FTP, gopher, and similarly
unsupported protocols are skipped outright.


RANKING
-------

A single probe is noisy — one slow DNS lookup or a momentary
network blip shouldn't rank a genuinely fast mirror below one that
just happened to get lucky. To avoid that, ranking happens in two
phases.

The first phase probes every candidate mirror once, exactly as
described above. From those results, the most promising mirrors
(up to 3x the requested count) move on to a second phase, where
each is re-probed 3 times at higher concurrency. The median of
those 3 samples becomes that mirror's real latency for ranking
purposes, rather than trusting a single reading.

A mirror that fails every re-probe in phase two is dropped as
unreachable. One that succeeds only some of the time is still
ranked, but its failures count as a heavy penalty, so a flaky
mirror is pushed toward the bottom rather than erased or scored
only on its best moments.

--rank-all applies this same refinement to its fastest 30 mirrors;
anything beyond that keeps its single-probe latency from phase one,
since refining every reachable mirror on an unfiltered run would be
expensive for little practical benefit.

This is not configurable — it is a fixed part of how the tool
ranks mirrors, not something the person running it needs to decide.


SAFETY
------

    - Never writes to disk without --update.
    - Never elevates privileges itself; you must invoke it with
      sudo. If run with --update and not root, it fails immediately
      before doing any network work.
    - --mirrors-only lets the tool run entirely rootless, printing
      only the raw mirrorlist to stdout so it can be piped into your
      own privileged write command instead.
    - Always backs up the existing mirrorlist before overwriting it.
    - Confirmation is required before any write, unless --force is
      explicitly passed.
    - --mirrors-only and --update cannot be combined; --exclude-country
      and --country-only cannot be combined; --ipv4-only and
      --ipv6-only cannot be combined.
    - --ipv4-only / --ipv6-only apply consistently across all
      protocols, including rsync.


BUILDING
--------

    go mod tidy
    go build -o barch-rankmirrors ./cmd/barch-rankmirrors


